import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Plan 0031 — measurement infrastructure: discovery benchmark, players-as-modules, baseline registry","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Plan 0031 — measurement infrastructure: discovery benchmark, players-as-modules, baseline registry","type":"plan","date":"2026-10-20T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/plans/0031-measurement-infra.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/plans/0031-measurement-infra.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/plans/0031-measurement-infra.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="plan-0031-—-measurement-infrastructure-discovery-benchmark-players-as-modules-baseline-registry" tabindex="-1">Plan 0031 — measurement infrastructure: discovery benchmark, players-as-modules, baseline registry <a class="header-anchor" href="#plan-0031-—-measurement-infrastructure-discovery-benchmark-players-as-modules-baseline-registry" aria-label="Permalink to “Plan 0031 — measurement infrastructure: discovery benchmark, players-as-modules, baseline registry”">​</a></h1><p>Origin: Lotufo review (2026-10-19). Two questions exposed the gap the suite must close — &quot;how do you know the cost?&quot; and &quot;how do you know it found something <em>better</em>?&quot; — and the honest answer needs a machine behind it, not a paragraph.</p><p><strong>Cost has three trust levels, and the suite exercises all three:</strong> extraction ranks on <code>dag_cost</code> (analytical: FLOPs + param bytes); admission measures pay on real seeded fixtures (wall / peak memory); delivery verifies the lowered module numerically against the source (fp64). &quot;Found a better algorithm&quot; = certified-equal output at lower measured cost. The scoreboard is analytical, the admission is measured, the output is verified.</p><h2 id="workstream-1-—-the-discovery-benchmark" tabindex="-1">Workstream 1 — the discovery benchmark <a class="header-anchor" href="#workstream-1-—-the-discovery-benchmark" aria-label="Permalink to “Workstream 1 — the discovery benchmark”">​</a></h2><p>A versioned corpus of <em>named</em> optimizations sourced from literature and real compilers — the suite the pitch implies exists.</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>bench/discovery/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  cases/&lt;name&gt;.toml     # spelled fixture + source citation + category + baselines</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  refs/&lt;name&gt;/          # known-good impls (RetNet chunked, opt_einsum, …)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Each case: spelled &quot;before&quot; module · citation · category (<code>memory | speed | structure | precision</code>) · registered baselines (eager, torch.compile, known impl). Runner <code>python -m mlopt_discovery.benchmark</code> emits per case:</p><ul><li>found? certified? (derivability + gauntlet outcome)</li><li>cost vs each baseline (symbolic and/or measured)</li><li><strong>beat-best-registered?</strong> — a certified form below the registered floor on a category = a real find, not a rediscovery. This is the novelty detector.</li></ul><p>Two lanes: <code>--fast</code> (symbolic only, seconds — PR gate) and <code>--full</code> (measured pay — nightly). Emits <code>docs/scorecard.md</code> + JSON side-file. First cases: conv_bn_fold, RetNet chunkwise, FlashAttention combine, Blelloch scan, mul_unsq_nat, weight-chain fold, swiglu_mlp.</p><h2 id="workstream-2-—-players-as-modules" tabindex="-1">Workstream 2 — players as modules <a class="header-anchor" href="#workstream-2-—-players-as-modules" aria-label="Permalink to “Workstream 2 — players as modules”">​</a></h2><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>packages/mlopt-discovery/players/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  _base.py                  # protocol: feature schema + move enumerator + policy</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  linear/  neural/  distilled/  playbook/  random/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    policy.py  train.py  &lt;artifact&gt;  README.md (measured record)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Registry maps name → spec; <code>--player &lt;name&gt;</code> on the play CLI. Adding a player = adding a folder. Comparison runner <code>python -m mlopt_discovery.compare --players … --boards …</code> replays the memoized-verdict protocol from <code>lab/policy_baseline.py</code> and emits <code>docs/leaderboard_players.md</code> — versioned rows (date, sha, seed) with metrics <em>and</em> a notes column (drain artifacts, observability walls — the interesting comparisons have been qualitative).</p><h2 id="workstream-3-—-baselines-same-shape" tabindex="-1">Workstream 3 — baselines, same shape <a class="header-anchor" href="#workstream-3-—-baselines-same-shape" aria-label="Permalink to “Workstream 3 — baselines, same shape”">​</a></h2><p><code>baselines/{name}/</code> mirroring players: opt_einsum, torch.compile, FLA, hand-written carriers — adapter + measured record each. The scorecard/leaderboard then read opponents from one registry, so the &quot;limits&quot; tables in results docs are <em>generated</em>, not hand-copied.</p><h2 id="order" tabindex="-1">Order <a class="header-anchor" href="#order" aria-label="Permalink to “Order”">​</a></h2><p>1 → 2 → 3, independent landings, nothing breaks between. Benchmark first because it is the referee for every future claim.</p>`,16)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};